-</head><body data-publish-at="2026-08-23T18:00:00Z"><nav class="navbar scrolled" id="navbar"><div class="nav-inner"><a class="brand" href="/index.html#home"><img class="brand-logo" src="/icons/brand-mark-v3.png" alt="" aria-hidden="true"><span>DAILY DOSE</span></a><div class="nav-links"><a href="/index.html#home">Home</a><a href="/story.html">About</a><a href="/devotions.html">Devotions</a><a href="/series.html">Series</a><a href="/verse-library.html">Verse Library</a><a href="/prayer">Prayer</a><a href="mailto:dailydosedevotions@gmail.com">Contact</a></div><button class="menu-btn" id="menuBtn" aria-label="Open menu">&#9776;</button></div></nav><main class="devotion-shell series-shell"><div class="container"><a class="back-link" href="/series/blessed-are.html">&larr; Back to Blessed Are</a><article class="devotion-article reveal visible"><p class="eyebrow">Daily Dose: Blessed Are</p><h1>Blessed Are: The Poor In Spirit - Part 1</h1><div class="devotion-meta">Releases Sunday Aug 23, 2026 at 7:00PM</div><div class="devotion-body formed-body"><div class="scripture-box"><h3>Matthew 5:3 (ESV)</h3><p><strong>&ldquo;Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.&rdquo;</strong></p></div><p>Jesus begins the Beatitudes in a way that challenges the world's usual way of thinking. He does not begin with power, confidence, success, influence, or outward strength. He begins with need. He begins with a heart that has stopped pretending it can stand before God on its own.</p><p>To be poor in spirit does not mean a person is worthless. It means they understand their spiritual poverty apart from God. It is the posture of someone who has stopped trusting in their own goodness, ability, reputation, or strength to save them. It is the honest confession: &ldquo;Lord, I need Your mercy.&rdquo;</p><p>The world teaches people to hide weakness. Independence and self-sufficiency are often praised. But that way of living becomes exhausting. There comes a point when pretending everything is fine becomes too heavy, distractions cannot quiet the heart, and success cannot heal what is broken inside.</p><p><strong>Psalm 51:17 (ESV)</strong> says, <strong>&ldquo;The sacrifices of God are a broken spirit; a broken and contrite heart, O God, you will not despise.&rdquo;</strong> David understood that God was not looking for outward performance while the heart remained proud and distant. God desired honesty, humility, repentance, and surrender.</p><p>Peter also learned this lesson. He believed his loyalty was stronger than everyone else's, yet fear exposed his weakness. His self-confidence collapsed, but that brokenness became the beginning of deeper dependence on grace.</p><p>This Beatitude is the gateway to the others. A person cannot truly mourn over sin, hunger for righteousness, walk in mercy, pursue purity, make peace, or endure persecution for Christ without first acknowledging their need for Him.</p><p>Jesus calls the poor in spirit blessed because they are no longer building their lives on themselves. Their hope is not pride, status, performance, or worldly identity. Their hope is the mercy and grace of God.</p></div><div class="devotion-nav"><a class="btn outline small" href="/series/blessed-are.html">Back to Blessed Are</a><a class="btn primary small" href="/devotions.html">Read Daily Dose</a></div></article></div></main><script src="/script-brand-share-v6.js"></script><script src="/pwa.js?v=pwa-4"></script></body></html>
