put him in the stocks.&rdquo;</strong></p><p>The apostles also lived this truth after the resurrection of Christ. <strong>Acts 5:41 (ESV)</strong> says, <strong>&ldquo;Then they left the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for the name.&rdquo;</strong></p><p>Jesus Himself is the perfect example. Though He was without sin, He was rejected, falsely accused, mocked, beaten, abandoned, and crucified. <strong>1 Peter 2:23 (ESV)</strong> says, <strong>&ldquo;When he was reviled, he did not revile in return,&rdquo;</strong> and <strong>&ldquo;continued entrusting himself to him who judges justly.&rdquo;</strong></p><p>The Beatitudes begin with surrender and end with endurance. They show how the kingdom of God changes people from the inside out, and Jesus assures His people that no act of faithfulness done for Him is forgotten by the Father.</p></div><div class="devotion-nav"><a class="btn outline small" href="/series/blessed-are.html">Back to Blessed Are</a><a class="btn primary small" href="/devotions.html">Read Daily Dose</a></div></article></div></main><script src="/script-brand-share-v6.js"></script><script src="/pwa.js?v=pwa-4"></script></body></html>
